00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
we've all seen the adverts pretty much  everyone on the internet is telling you  to leave your job and go work for  yourself if you're not already side  hustling your way to a million you  should flip your soyboy boss off as you  hand in your resignation to carve out  your own destiny but there are some huge  dark sides to running your own business  that the hustle and grind world will  never tell you about so allow me to piss  on this parade here are 10 reasons why  working for yourself  absolutely sucks number one you are only  ever accountable to yourself not having  to answer to a boss sounds fantastic no  more pointless tuesday morning meetings  and no one whinging if you return late  from lunch  but that accountability provided  structure now  not only are you the guy doing the work  you're the guy telling the guy to do the  work so  no one is going to tell you off if that  email doesn't get sent on time or that  presentation is a little bit over budget  the only person who pays that price is  you  and your business  and your accounts and your long-term  financial prospects after 15 years of  running businesses i can tell you that  being both the organ grinder and the  monkey is exhausting and flip-flopping  from neuroticism to apathy  isn't a good situation number two never  knowing when you're finished many people  start their own business to take control  of their time but being in charge of  your own workload means that there's  always something else to complete  instead of having a prescribed list of  tasks to move through before 5 pm  now you have a never-ending conveyor  belt of things to do over time your life  can become invaded by a permanent sense  that there's unfinished work  this ambient to-do list anxiety may well  help you to stay competitive but that  doesn't mean it will actually increase  your quality of life over time your  typical finish goes from five to six to  seven pm  and it's very hard to turn around once  you've started down that road  this can turn into a vicious cycle of  mounting workloads and increasing guilt  leading to more work and more guilt and  so on and so on the price that you pay  in missed events time with friends and  family and just opportunities to  experience life is real yes  you are your own boss at work now but  your work has become the real boss of  your life number three attaching your  sense of self-worth to the success of  the business i spent most of my 20s  feeling great when the businesses were  doing well and terrible when they were  doing badly even though most of their  performance was out of my control this  is a recipe for disaster you can do  all the things correctly you can send  the emails on time and come up with  awesome marketing campaigns and create  the right product  and the market responds in the way the  market responds especially if you're a  young entrepreneur this is a real risk  you're basically outsourcing your sense  of happiness  and well-being and confidence to a third  party that you have a negligible control  over previously there was a buffer right  between your work and its outcomes but  now it feels like a direct relationship  if you are what you do  then your value is how well you do it  you care about your business so  obviously you want to do something that  makes you feel proud and fulfilled but  very quickly this can turn from  emotional investment into identity  attachment number four permanently  having to create your own motivation in  a normal job you externalize motivation  to your colleagues and your boss and the  company rules but this support system is  no longer there even if you're starting  a business with colleagues and employees  the book stops with you now if you don't  do it it won't get done and no one but  yourself can motivate you this can  become  absolutely exhausting now not only do  you need the motivation to do the work  you need the motivation to tell yourself  to do the work  this strains your willpower reserves for  other things not only in your business  but outside of it too your motivation to  train hard or stick to a diet or build a  new habit will suffer because of how  much effort you need to expend just to  keep your business going number five  holidays and downtime barely exist i  spent  204 saturdays in a row setting up the  same club night and never took a holiday  which lasted longer than four days until  i was 26 because i was terrified of  leaving my business for too long i only  went on reality tv because they promised  me that they could get me back to our  event ready for the doors to open on a  saturday night  your competitive advantage comes from  noticing things that your competitors  don't and being more detailed and  creative and consistent this is the  superpower of a small agile lean startup  but it's really hard to switch off  even if you do manage to shoehorn some  time away in that doesn't mean that your  mind will be on vacation as well you  will be on holiday somewhere warm and  sunny and lovely and relaxing thinking  about a project that you need to finish  when you get back to your laptop also  genuine creativity comes from stepping  away from the tasks that you need to do  the reason that you were probably good  in the first place is that you were able  to see things that the rest of the  market wasn't if you're not careful you  can spend so much time ruminating about  work that you never get the distance  needed to actually be creative number  six never being certain if what you're  working on next is the right thing when  you're both the person who makes the  rules and follows the rules you can get  caught up spending a lot of time  questioning instead of executing there  are no standard operating procedures for  you to follow usually if you had a  question about what to do next you just  ask your boss this outsources the  decision-making and the culpability for  getting it wrong  to them now you don't have that free  pass even after a while when you've  created a typical working routine the  fact that you made the routine will  always leave you with this distracting  sense that  maybe you should be doing something else  number seven no assurances about income  another reason people start their own  business  is because they want to be in control of  making their own money no longer are  your efforts and your outcomes detached  from each other now you take on the risk  and receive the reward but there are  no assurances here the market does not  give a single [ __ ] about you it doesn't  care how hard you've worked you can do  absolutely everything right and watch  your revenue get chopped in half next  month some months you'll double your  income  and some months you'll make nothing you  need to ask yourself whether you can  handle this  really think about it in a normal job  you'll receive your paycheck no matter  how the company is performing  some people are built for this  monetary orderliness they're like stable  linear progression that they can predict  and it makes them feel safe if you  strike out on your own you become your  own pension backup plan holiday  allowance sick leave and pay rise  all rolled into one watching your  financial security get ragged around at  the mercy of market forces  isn't fun number eight it becomes much  harder to get a mortgage buying a house  or building a property portfolio is on  many people's lists of financial goals  if you're in a typical employment and  have about 25 to put down you can  usually find a bank to lend you around  five times your annual salary to buy a  house if you're self-employed however  the bank will scrutinize your accounts  much more closely in the uk you'll need  to provide three years of sa 302s which  are the self-assessments of annual  income plus they may even dig deeper  into the sources of that to look at  wages dividends and any other forms of  income that you've received i don't know  how it works in america  but i can't imagine it's any prettier  this means you essentially cannot get a  mortgage if you've been self-employed  for less than three years that might not  sound like long but if you're holding up  your ability to enter the market or move  into a dream home it will feel like an  eternity number nine  paperwork  even if you have a huge team and a [ __ ]  hot assistant you will have to deal with  more paperwork realistically you'll  probably have to do most if not all of  it yourself articles of incorporation  bank accounts trademarks leases business  rates and then constant ongoing checks  of these is beyond tedious yes  it's cool to have a bank account with  your company name on it but no one talks  about the headaches of submitting your  annual expenses every year or dealing  with inquiries from her majesty's  revenue and customs if you're  administratively minded this may be fine  for you but if you're already cutting it  tight to meet the deadline finding out  at 3pm on a friday that you've got an  intellectual property dispute to deal  with is an ugly situation number 10  always feeling like your work could have  been better  you probably want to start your business  because you're passionate and talented  at something but if there is one way to  turn a love into a labor it's to  monetize it you don't know what time  you're supposed to finish or  specifically how you're supposed to do  the job and you don't take any time off  from doing it but even if you get past  all that you are now the final quality  control check for your own work  and everyone else's the amount of time  that you will spend double and triple  checking work cannot be understated you  will wake up in the middle of the night  sweating because you realize that a  slide is missing from tomorrow's  presentation or you'll need to hotspot  off a tour guide's phone to update a  link on the website this  is the entry price you pay for being at  the top of the tree  the book will always stop with you so  the question you need to ask is  why are you thinking of working for  yourself presumably you want to be happy  and satisfied with your job but all of  the gains you make in increased freedom  and money and input each come with an  associated cost you will use up  willpower and spare time and sanity to  make this happen you need to work out if  that's truly a price that you're willing  to pay thank you very much for tuning in  press here to watch my video on what  happened when i stopped drinking for 1  000 days if you're wanting to start your  own business you'll need to be more  productive and taking a break from  alcohol is a really good place to begin  and don't forget to subscribe  peace

